Do you put together in your mind the work you &apos;re doing , the future of Africa , this conference — what connections can we make , if any , between them ?
Kwabena Boahen : Yes , like I said at the beginning , I got my first computer when I was a teenager , growing up in Accra .
And I had this gut reaction that this was the wrong way to do it .
It was very brute force ; it was very inelegant .
I don &apos;t think that I would &apos;ve had that reaction , if I &apos;d grown up reading all this science fiction , hearing about RD2D2 , whatever it was called , and just — you know , buying into this hype about computers .
I was coming at it from a different perspective , where I was bringing that different perspective to bear on the problem .
And I think a lot of people in Africa have this different perspective , and I think that &apos;s going to impact technology .
And that &apos;s going to impact how it &apos;s going to evolve .
And I think you &apos;re going to be able to see , use that infusion , to come up with new things , because you &apos;re coming from a different perspective .
I think we can contribute . We can dream like everybody else .
